2026-10-13 — K04: replay blocked; source-bound narrowing, NOT closure.

Replay status. The original stress_04 pair cannot run on any retained
kernel. Its GCC/EvoCC Images (13bd4d0b..., 62c29ae4..., and the later
22a87df2... / 18b36fee... composition), runtime disks and kselftest payloads
lived under .build/k7-coverage, which was retired on October 7. A search of
.build, .build/case and .evidence finds no copy. The two current Images
(GCC b6f206dea584..., EvoCC add1c4874e28...) are virtconfig builds with
"# CONFIG_BLK_DEV_UBLK is not set". This slice may not build a kernel, so
the original pair, its repeated replays and their load records are NOT_RUN.
No timeout, watchdog, workload or verdict is changed.

Retained full-campaign captures of the unchanged stress_04 (hvf-host,
.evidence/kselftest-full/RUN/{reference,subject}/ublk/test_stress_04.sh/):
  run                          GCC reference          EvoCC subject
  20260914T121718Z             TIMEOUT (2dde028e)     PASS (df7ca716)
  20260922T033617Z             PASS (8fa43fb4)        TIMEOUT (1d4890fa)
  seven-differences-serial-0923 PASS (8fa43fb4)       TIMEOUT (f2d59e05)
  20260927T223104Z             TIMEOUT (13bd4d0b)     PASS (62c29ae4)
Each TIMEOUT is "not ok 1 ... # TIMEOUT 45 seconds", then EVO_INIT_DONE and
no power-off before the boot watchdog. The 20260914T015846Z pair is SKIP on
both sides and is not counted. GCC and EvoCC each time out in 2 of 4.
ublk-blocked-observer.out (September 28, GCC) adds the blocked path:
writeback workers for 259:5 and 259:8 wait in blk_mq_get_tag at 25 s, 40 s
and before the shutdown sync, and fio/io_uring readers wait for folios.
Its other 24 observed original invocations (12 per kernel, September
27-28) all pass.

Source path (Linux 6.18.44, the pinned tree; hashes below):
  test_stress_04.sh starts 11 background jobs on null, loop and stripe
  targets; 7 of them use -z or --auto_zc. Each job runs buffered fio
  (libaio and io_uring, randrw, depth 256, numjobs=nproc), sleeps 2 s, then
  test_common.sh:244 __ublk_kill_daemon sends kill -9 and polls the state
  once a second, up to 50 times, until it reads DEAD.
  ublk_drv.c:1726 ublk_ch_release_work_fn runs after the daemon closes the
  char device. For zero-copy and auto-buffer devices, 1746 reschedules the
  work every jiffy while ublk_check_and_reset_active_ref sees a live I/O
  buffer reference; those drop only when the daemon's io_uring frees its
  registered buffers. The work then aborts server-owned requests, sets
  force_abort and calls ublk_stop_dev_unlocked (1804). That calls
  del_gendisk at 2109; only after it returns does ublk_detach_disk (2084)
  set state DEAD and ublksrv_pid -1 (2092).
  Therefore a device whose release work keeps waiting for a buffer
  reference, or whose del_gendisk cannot finish, never reads DEAD. The kill
  loop then runs into the 45-second limit, and the dirty pages of that disk
  keep the final sync waiting. The captured flush workers in
  blk_mq_get_tag fit this path: the queue's tags are held by requests that
  never complete. Which request stays incomplete, and why, is NOT captured.

kublk's 10-second close fallback is not on this path:
  kublk.c:1092 ublk_stop_io_daemon returns at 1099 when ublksrv_pid < 0.
  stress_04 calls "kublk del" only after the kill loop has read DEAD, and
  DEAD comes with ublksrv_pid = -1, so the IN_CLOSE wait at 1113 is skipped.
  The fallback can run only when del starts before DEAD. Then kill(pid, 0)
  at 1103 still succeeds for a zombie: kublk double-forks (1276-1288), the
  killed daemon is reparented to PID 1, and tools/k1/selftest-init.c reaps
  only its direct child until a program ends. So a deletion that starts
  before DEAD can wait 10 s. The shared-backing GCC deadline in
  ublk-kill-cancel.out had no trace, so its path stays unknown. The
  fallback cannot explain the original 45-second TIMEOUT or the shutdown
  stall.

Upstream, checked 2026-10-13: no report of a stress_04 hang or a
blk_mq_get_tag stall after a killed ublk server was found. Mainline ublk
fixes since 6.18 (for example 0842186d2c4e, 845db023a8ae, f7700a4415af,
1133b93fc7f6, 432a9b2780c0, 8a14be55bdc6) address recovery, fetch and
cancel races or use-after-free; none describes this path, and none is tested
here. The 6.18 stable branch after 6.18.44 (through 2026-10-11) has ublk,
blk-mq and io_uring fixes, none of which names this hang.

Disposition: the stall is NOT EvoCC-specific. The GCC reference shows it as
often as the EvoCC subject, and its path runs through ublk teardown,
del_gendisk and blk-mq tag waits. This classifies the observed failure as
shared; it does not demonstrate a kernel cause.
K04's exit (the original workload with clean shutdown, or a source-bound
disposition) is NOT met: the incomplete request and the holder of the
missing buffer reference or tag are not identified.

Next evidence needed, after the owner restores or approves a ublk-enabled
pair: on a stalled run, sample the exit_work state (scheduled delayed work),
each ublk_io's flags and reference count, io_uring io_ring_exit_work stacks
and the per-hctx tag owners, before and after the kill loop. The original
45-second limit stays unchanged.

